# Cross-platform Rust

Single source for platform conventions; other rules and skills link here.

## Platforms

| Target | Status |
|--------|--------|
| Linux X11 | Shipped (`x11_*` in `sqyre-capture`, rdev hotkeys) |
| Linux Wayland | Shipped. Portal ScreenCast+PipeWire capture and RemoteDesktop EIS input need `portal-capture` (Linux Makefile targets enable it); foreign-toplevel/AT-SPI windows and layer-shell outline/grab always build; evdev hotkeys need `hooks` (`linux/wayland/`, `linux/session.rs`). Per-desktop gaps: `linux-desktop-parity` |
| Windows | Bare `.exe`: GDI capture, outline, focus, low-level hooks (`win_*`) |
| macOS | Capture/focus stubbed; `make macos` host-only |
| wasm32 | GUI-only editor, no runtime (see `wasm-editor`) |
| Android | Scaffold: MediaProjection capture, accessibility gestures, app-launch focus via `sqyre-android` (only JNI crate); app picker, SAF document picks, notification Stop/Continue, static Tesseract; no keys, hooks, overlay, or folder picks (`docs/ANDROID.md`). `target_os = "android"` is not `linux` but is `unix` |

Ship formats: Linux AppImage + Flatpak, Windows `.exe`, WASM zip. No MSI/DMG/Nix unless asked.

## `#[cfg]` vs `cfg!`

- `#[cfg(...)]` removes code at compile time; `cfg!(...)` is a runtime bool and still type-checks on every target.
- Never call platform-only APIs inside `if cfg!(...)`; use `#[cfg]` modules or blocks.
- Platform imports live inside the `#[cfg]` block/module that uses them; do not paper over with `#[allow(unused_imports)]`.
- Cargo: `[target.'cfg(...)'.dependencies]` for OS crates (`x11`, `windows`, `tray-icon`, …).
- Use family predicates (`unix` / `windows`) only when behavior truly matches; usually split `target_os = "linux"` / `"windows"` / `"macos"`.

```rust
// CORRECT
#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
fn capture() { /* Win32 */ }

// WRONG — still type-checked on Linux
fn capture() {
    if cfg!(target_os = "windows") { /* Win32 APIs */ }
}
```

## Features and targets

- Features are additive (Cargo book): enabling one only adds code. No mutually exclusive features; no `cfg(not(feature = …))` that changes enabled behavior; no `wasm`/`x11`/`wayland` features — use `cfg(target_*)` and runtime session detection.
- Custom cfgs must be declared (`[lints.rust] unexpected_cfgs` `check-cfg` or `cargo::rustc-check-cfg`).
- A Linux build only proves Linux cfgs. Touching `win_*`/`cfg(windows)` → `make windows` (or say it wasn't compiled); `portal-capture` code → `--features portal-capture`; editor-reachable code → `make wasm-check`.

## FFI (Win32 / X11 / PipeWire)

- `windows` crate only (no `winapi`). Own each handle (`HDC`, `HBITMAP`, `Display*`, `XImage`, PipeWire fds/buffers) in an RAII guard whose `Drop` releases it, so `?`/early returns cannot leak (see `X11State`, `MappedFd`).
- Keep `unsafe` inside the platform module behind safe fns; `// SAFETY:` per `rust-style`.

## Public surface

- One OS-neutral API across targets: `OsCapturer`, `shared_capturer`, `SharedRunCapturer`, `OsWindowFocuser` (never `X11Capturer`-style names).
- Shared traits (`ScreenCapturer`, `WindowFocuser`, `AutomationBackend`) stay OS-agnostic.
- Wayland vs X11 is a runtime session choice (`linux/session.rs`) behind the same surface, not a parallel API.
- Extend existing `#[cfg]` modules instead of inventing parallel stacks; no dual codecs or compatibility shims.
